 "existing_tips": [
  "\"Det vill säga\" (dvs.) = that is (i.e.).",
  "Direct \"jag vill ha\" is casual but common among friends.",
  "\"Låna\" means both to borrow and to lend – context determines which.",
  "\"Borde\" = should/ought to. It implies advice or mild obligation.",
  "\"Kort\" = card. Sweden is nearly cashless – cards accepted everywhere.",
  "Swish is Sweden's mobile payment app – used everywhere from stores to street vendors.",
  "'Med' (with) – companionship or means: 'med familjen' (with family), 'med tåg' (by train).",
  "'Få' (may/get to) – dual meaning: 'Du får gå' (You may go) or 'Jag får en present' (I get a gift).",
  "\"Får jag\" = may I. Polite but not overly formal.",
  "\"Ska\" for plans/arrangements: \"Vi ska träffas\" = we're meeting (arranged).",
  "\"Vill ha\" = want (a thing). Just \"vill\" = want (to do). Don't forget \"ha\" with nouns.",
  "Modal + inte + infinitive: the negation goes between the modal and main verb."
 ]
}